Ed McCaskey, played by David Huddleston.

9
00:34:34,736 --> 00:34:40,736
Brian's Song is a 1971 ABC Movie of the Week that recounts the life of Brian Piccolo (James Caan), a Chicago Bears football
player stricken with terminal cancer, focusing on his friendship with teammate Gale Sayers (Billy Dee Williams).

10
00:38:25,263 --> 00:38:31,263
Piccolo's and Sayers's sharply differing temperaments and racial backgrounds made them unlikely to become friends but
they did, becoming the first interracial roommates in the history of the National Football League.

11
00:42:15,789 --> 00:42:21,789
The film chronicles the evolution of their friendship,
ending with Piccolo's death in 1970.

12
00:46:06,315 --> 00:46:12,315
The production was such a success on ABC that it was later shown in theaters by Columbia Pictures
with a major premiere in Chicago; however, it was soon withdrawn for lack of business.

13
00:49:56,842 --> 00:50:02,842
Critics have called the movie one of the finest television movies ever made.

14
00:53:47,368 --> 00:53:53,368
A 2005 readers' poll taken by Entertainment Weekly ranked Brian's
Song seventh in its list of the top "guy-cry" films.

15
00:57:37,894 --> 00:57:43,894
The movie is based on Sayers's account of his friendship with Piccolo and coping
with Piccolo's illness in Sayers's 1970 autobiography, I Am Third.

16
01:01:28,421 --> 01:01:34,421
The film was written by William Blinn, whose script one Dallas television critic called "highly restrained, steering clear of
any overt sentimentality [yet conveying] the genuine affection the two men felt so deeply for each other."

17
01:05:18,947 --> 01:05:24,947
The film explores themes of friendship, sports,
american football, terminal illness, cancer.

18
01:09:09,473 --> 01:09:15,473
The tone is emotional.
